[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Data Types, Storage Layout, and Memory Management in Solidity"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding how Solidity handles data types, storage layout, and memory management is crucial for writing gas-efficient and secure smart contracts. Ethereum Virtual Machine (EVM) is a stack-based machine with specific data locations."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Solidity Data Types"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Solidity is a statically typed language, meaning the type of each variable must be specified at compile time. Data types are broadly categorized into value types and reference types."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Value Types: These are types that are always passed by value, meaning they are copied when they are used as function arguments or in assignments. Examples include booleans, integers (uint8 to uint256), addresses, and fixed-size byte arrays."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reference Types: These types share a data location. They include arrays, structs, and mappings. Because they can be larger, their location (storage, memory, or calldata) must be explicitly stated."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Data Locations"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Every complex reference type (arrays, structs) must have an explicitly annotated data location. There are three primary locations in Solidity:"},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Storage: Where state variables are kept. Persistent across function calls and extremely expensive to read and write."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Memory: Temporary holding place for data during a function execution. Cleared after the function call ends. Costs are much lower than storage."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Calldata: A non-modifiable, non-persistent area where function arguments are stored. It behaves similarly to memory and is the cheapest place for external function inputs."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Storage Layout Fundamentals"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"The EVM storage is a key-value store with 2^256 slots, where each slot is 32 bytes (256 bits) wide. State variables are packed tightly into these slots sequentially starting from slot 0."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"contract StorageLayoutExample {
    uint128 a; // Slot 0 (16 bytes)
    uint128 b; // Slot 0 (16 bytes) - packed together
    uint256 c; // Slot 1 (takes a full 32-byte slot)
}"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, variables a and b fit into a single 32-byte slot because their combined size is 32 bytes (16 bytes each). However, variable c requires a fresh slot because it cannot fit into the remaining space of slot 0."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Storage Packing Optimization"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"To minimize gas costs associated with SLOAD and SSTORE operations, developers should order state variables efficiently. Grouping smaller variables (like uint8, uint128, bool) together so they fit into 32-byte boundaries saves substantial gas."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Memory Management and Expansion"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Memory is allocated linearly and starts at offset 0x40. Solidity maintains the 'free memory pointer' at this location. Unlike high-level languages, memory in Solidity cannot be freed explicitly; it grows during a transaction, and gas costs increase quadratically once memory usage passes a certain threshold."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"contract MemoryExample {
    function processArray(uint256[] memory inputData) public pure returns (uint256) {
        uint256[] memory localArray = new uint256[](5);
        localArray[0] = inputData[0];
        return localArray[0];
    }
}"},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mastering storage layout optimization and understanding memory costs ensures your smart contracts remain scalable, secure, and cost-effective for end users."}]
